:MILD_120022 a owl:Class;
  rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OGMS_0000045>, _:genid37, _:genid38;
  owl:disjointWith :MILD_120023, :MILD_120024;
  <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> "a disorder is an abnormal presence or organisation of material components — including the limiting case where the abnormal organisation consists in a normally-present component being absent."@en;
  <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/description> "Pathologicity is asserted - not inferred -  by primitives (e.g. threat to life, pain/suffering, loss of ability/fertility) as mentioned by Hucklenbroich et al."@en;
  rdfs:comment "A pathological disorder should not be identified with a merely voluntary, transient, and immediately reversible functional deviation. In substance use disorder cases, the candidate pathological disorder is the relatively stable neurobiological or neuropsychological organization that grounds impaired control, craving, tolerance, withdrawal tendency, or harmful recurrence, not a single act of substance use."@en,
    "ExO_0000079 is reused as a comparison anchor rather than as the MILD disease definition: pathological disorder may involve abnormal functioning, abnormal localization of normal functioning, or abnormal localization/organization of constituents relative to relevant members of the same species. Species-comparative abnormality is not sufficient by itself, because rare variation is not automatically pathological."@en;
  rdfs:label "pathological disorder"@en;
  <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "a disorder is an abnormal presence or organisation of material components — including the limiting case where the abnormal organisation consists in a normally-present component being absent."@en .

